From 3ea3b14dd021ecc0e5b6237af1c23f33942a363e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lmoresi <louis.moresi@anu.edu.au>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 21:04:30 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] A fill ring that crosses the band's skin is refused before
 meshing; so is a seam connector that would cut the band

On the seam-conforming path at np 3 (an annulus, the band's top at 60
degrees) one rank's fill ring held a straight connector, 5.4 cells long,
from a wall vertex across the band's tip. The seam skirted the tip without
crossing it, its vertices were carved, and the connector that closes such a
span cut through the band. The check before meshing tested the ring only
against itself, so gmsh got the ring, and spun on it while the other ranks
waited in the placement's gather.

- Before every fill the ring is tested against its holes (the band's skin):
  no proper crossing, with the tolerant orientation test of the self-crossing
  count (a hole touching the ring at a vertex or along an edge is not one),
  and no hole outside the ring. The refusal is raised inside the placement's
  failure gather, so every rank refuses and none meshes.
- The seam-conforming loop builder tests a span's straight connector against
  the band, and refuses when it would cut it, naming seams='gather'.

test_0909 (tier B): the hung rank's ring and skin (dumped) are refused, two
crossings and the tip outside; holes inside, touching at a vertex and along
an edge pass; a hole outside is refused; the loop builder refuses a
connector across a band tip and closes one clear of it.

Underworld development team with AI support from Claude Code
